data.content:
Here's the complete migration. The original code uses three patterns that don't exist in modern .NET:

| Old Pattern | Problem in .NET 8 | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.End()` | Called `Thread.Abort` internally → `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Return an `IActionResult` |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `true` triggered `Thread.Abort` | Return `Redirect()` result |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown in .NET 8 | Remove entirely |

## Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core / .NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // Was: btnExport_Click event handler with Response.Write + Response.End
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // Return a FileResult — the framework handles headers and
        // content-disposition. No Response.Clear/Write/End needed.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    // Was: btnRedirect_Click with Response.Redirect(url, true)
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult ProcessAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Just return a redirect result — no thread abort involved.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // ThreadAbortException catch block removed entirely:
        // it is never thrown in .NET 8, so it was dead code.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls to Watch For

**1. Don't write to `Response` then return a result** — pick one or the other:
```csharp
// WRONG — mixing raw Response writes with IActionResult
Response.Headers.Append("Content-Disposition", "...");
Response.WriteAsync(data);
return Ok(); // throws: response already started

// RIGHT — let the framework handle it
return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
```

**2. Code after `Response.Redirect` now executes** — in Web Forms, `Response.Redirect(url, true)` aborted the thread so nothing after it ran. In ASP.NET Core, `Redirect()` returns a result object — code keeps executing. Always `return` it:
```csharp
// WRONG — ProcessRequest() still runs!
if (!User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
    Redirect("/login");
ProcessRequest();

// RIGHT
if (!User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
    return Redirect("/login");
```

**3. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` is dead code** — remove it. If your catch block had cleanup logic, move it to `finally`.

**4. Use `HttpContext.RequestAborted` for long-running work** — this is the ASP.NET Core equivalent of request termination:
```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> LongExport(CancellationToken ct)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted
    // to the CancellationToken parameter
    var data = await GenerateLargeReportAsync(ct);
    return File(data, "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

**5. Null-safety on `User.Identity`** — in ASP.NET Core, `User.Identity` can be `null` when no auth middleware is configured. Use `User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? false`.
